Why rucking works for weight loss

A 170-pound person walking unloaded at 3 mph on flat pavement burns roughly 280 calories an hour. Add a 25-pound pack at the same pace and that rises to about 310. The pack earns its keep on hills: the same 25 pounds up a 5% grade is about 485, and 35 pounds up an 8% grade is about 620, close to jogging at 5 mph, without the impact.

Those numbers come from the Pandolf load-carriage equation, the model behind our calorie calculator. A 2017 study by Drain and colleagues found it under-predicts energy use under heavy, fast military loads; at the light loads here, read the figures as estimates rather than a floor. The calculator methodology covers the model's limits. The practical lesson for weight loss: choose a hillier route before a heavier plate.

Impact is the other reason it suits weight loss. Walking keeps one foot on the ground at all times, so each step lands with far less force than a running stride. Rucking is lower impact than running at the same pace, though it is not impact-free, so build the load slowly. For anyone with sore knees or a history of running injuries, that difference can make a routine easier to keep up over the months it takes to change body composition.

Gear matters mostly in one way: a load that shifts or hangs low makes every walk less comfortable, and an uncomfortable routine is harder to keep.

The four-item starter kit

Rucking does not need ten items. It needs four. Everything else is optional or upgradeable later.

Buy in this order

1. A pack that holds weight properly

A common mistake is an old hiking daypack with no plate pocket and a soft back panel. The weight sags toward your lower back and shifts with every step.

What to look for:

A structured back panel (not floppy foam)

Either a dedicated plate pocket OR enough structure to hold a flexible weight flat

Padded shoulder straps (not flat nylon webbing)

Enough room for the weight, water and a layer; a bigger pack only helps if you can keep the weight from moving

Starter budget: CONDOR 3-Day Assault. Tactical, 50L, MOLLE, 600D polyester. Fine for moderate use while you learn what fit you prefer. It is bigger than you need, so secure the weight high against the back panel.

Recommended: 5.11 RUSH 24 2.0. 1050D water-repellent nylon, 37L, durable. The best balance of capacity, build, and price.

Premium: GORUCK Rucker 5.0. Dual elevated plate pockets, a 30 mm lumbar pad, a 1000D Cordura shell, and the SCARS lifetime guarantee. Buy this if you already know rucking is your sport.

Daily-carry premium: GORUCK GR1 26L. It adds broader daily and travel utility to the durable 1000D Cordura build and SCARS lifetime warranty. If you are choosing between a training-first Rucker and a work-and-travel GR1, see our full Rucker vs GR1 comparison.

2. The weight itself

Start with 5 to 10 lb for 20 to 30 minutes on level ground, whatever you weigh. Add time first, then weight in small steps.

Books or water bottles are fine for a first trial walk if you wrap them and wedge them high against the back panel. Loose bricks and sandbags shift and create pressure points, so once you know you will keep rucking, buy a plate or a flexible weight.

Budget: Yes4All cast-iron rucking weights. The 10 lb pair lets you start with one plate and add the second later, and a single 20 lb plate is also sold. Check the plate dimensions against your pack's sleeve.

Premium fit: GORUCK Ruck Plate, starting with the 10 lb Standard. It is sized for GORUCK's plate pockets.

Non-GORUCK pack? The flex pick: Hyperwear FlexLoad. Each fabric sleeve holds removable half-pound weights, so you can start light and add a little at a time. The sleeves are 12.25 inches tall and about 10 inches wide, so measure your pack first and secure the sleeve so it cannot slide.

3. Socks that reduce blister risk

Skip cotton, which holds sweat against the skin. Merino or a good synthetic blend handles sweat better and cuts friction.

Default: Darn Tough Light Hiker. A merino-nylon blend knit in Vermont, with Darn Tough's published lifetime guarantee.

Alternative: Smartwool Hike Full Cushion Crew. A softer full-cushion option that is widely stocked.

Toe-separation option: Injinji Trail Midweight. For anyone prone to inter-toe blisters.

4. Hydration

The only item where cheapest works great.

Simple bottle: Nalgene 32 oz Wide Mouth. Durable and sized for many pack side pockets.

Insulated upgrade: Hydro Flask 32 oz. It keeps water colder on long summer rucks.

The first four weeks

Progression matters as much as gear. The four weeks below get you started; for the full ramp, follow our 12-week rucking for weight loss plan.

Week 1: 20-30 minute rucks with 5-10 lb, 3x per week with a rest day between. Level ground, normal pace. Goal: get your feet and connective tissue used to load.

Week 2: Same weight, same frequency, bump to 35-40 minutes per ruck.

Week 3: If no knee or back pain, add 5 pounds. Stay at 40 minutes.

Week 4: If still feeling good, extend one ruck per week to 60 minutes. Keep the others at 40.

Red flags (back off immediately):

Sharp lumbar pain (not muscle fatigue)

Numbness or tingling in your hands or arms. Strap pressure on the nerves at the shoulder can cause rucksack palsy, so stop, loosen the straps, lighten the load, and see a doctor if it does not clear.

Knee pain that persists 24+ hours after a ruck

Any foot pain that is not a simple blister

After four weeks, keep adding time before weight, and add 5 lb only when the current load feels routine. For weight loss, a heavier plate is optional: as the numbers above show, a hillier route adds more work than extra pounds.

What you do not need to buy (yet)

These can wait:

GPS watch: your phone tracks pace and distance. Buy a watch once rucking sticks.

Trekking poles: not needed for flat pavement or gentle trails.

Hydration bladder: a water bottle in the side pocket works.

Plate carrier vest: the pack-and-plate combo is the beginner default.

Expensive tactical gear: reflective gear, headlamps, paracord, MOLLE accessories. Buy these when you have a specific use case.

The beginner starter kit (if you want it done for you)

If you would rather not assemble the kit yourself, use the beginner starter kit. It keeps the pack, weight, shoes, socks, hydration, and anti-chafe picks together so you can review each current product before buying.

It comes in Budget, Standard and Premium versions built from the same categories covered above. It is an editorial equipment selection, not a setup tested as a weight-loss intervention.

How rucking compares to other weight-loss cardio

Rucking and walking figures come from the Pandolf equation in our calculator. Running and cycling apply MET values from the 2024 Adult Compendium of Physical Activities to a 170 lb person: running at 8.5 and 9.3 METs as in our calculator, cycling at 6.8 to 8.0 METs for 10-14 mph leisure riding.

Rucking, cycling, and walking can all support sustainable weight loss. Rucking combines higher energy demand than unloaded walking with equipment that can last for years, but the best choice is still the one you can repeat consistently.

Start with 5 to 10 lb for 20 to 30 minutes on level ground. Add time before weight, then add 5 lb at a time once the current load feels routine. A heavier load is not required for weight loss; hills add more work than extra pounds.

More days do not guarantee faster fat loss. Begin with a schedule you can recover from and account for other training, load and symptoms. There is no universal sustainable maximum or inevitable injury outcome from frequency alone.

It depends mostly on your overall calorie balance. Rucking adds work but will not create a large deficit on its own: an hour with 25 lb is about 310 calories on flat ground and about 485 on a 5% grade for a 170 lb person, so three hour-long rucks a week add roughly 900 to 1,450 calories. Diet sets most of the deficit; rucking adds to it and keeps you active.

A cheaper pack is fine while you learn what fit and capacity you prefer. GORUCK's advantage is the lifetime guarantee and the elevated plate system on the current Rucker 5.0, both meaningful upgrades once you know rucking is sticking for you. Start with the pack you already own or a well-fitting budget pack; upgrade when your mileage and load justify it.

Running burns more per hour (about 670-790 calories at 5-6 mph for a 170 lb person, against about 310 for a flat ruck or 485 on a 5% grade), but each stride lands with much more force. If you already run without injury, mixing running and rucking works well. If your joints are sensitive or you are new to exercise, rucking is the gentler way to build a routine you can keep.

Start with comfortable walking or running shoes that already fit you well. For pavement, the Hoka Bondi 9 is a highly cushioned option; for mixed pavement and trail, the Saucony Peregrine 16 adds grip. Introduce unfamiliar minimalist or zero-drop footwear gradually rather than pairing a new shoe geometry with a new load on day one. Full pavement shoe roundup →

For cardio, it can. It is a light strength stimulus, though, and the Physical Activity Guidelines for Americans call for muscle-strengthening activity on at least two days a week alongside aerobic exercise. A practical split is two or three rucks plus two short strength sessions with bodyweight, bands or dumbbells.

